daemon: also act on the chip's wanted_pos resend hint while sock_can_send_data,
not only while sock_wait_usb_data

Background: chasing the SDIO-mode "bb_socket_write made no progress" stalls
(ar8030-transport-tx's own log line -- see this project's ar8030-transport
repo, tx/main.c) that cause visible video pauses under sustained load.
Live-captured the daemon's own debug-pad log (/var/log/ar8030/daemon_log/)
around a real stall and found dev_dat_so_write_proc()'s handling of the
chip's -0x108 reply ("wanted pos" -- an idle-notify telling the daemon
what position it should resend from if anything was dropped) firing
almost immediately after the drop it was reporting (~2ms later), well
before the client's own multi-second bb_socket_write() timeout. But the
handler only acted on it when psock->sock_sta == sock_wait_usb_data --
and _sock_dev_pack_make() (a different thread) hadn't necessarily flipped
sock_sta to that state yet when this reply landed. When the race lost,
the wantedpos hint was silently discarded and nothing else retried it:
the socket just sat there until some later, unrelated event (typically
the *next* client write, whose sock_dev_push_data() call happens to run
while sock_sta is sock_wait_usb_data) coincidentally re-checked the same
stale wantedpos -- observed on real hardware to take up to ~2.5s, which
lines up exactly with bb_socket_write()'s own client-side timeout window.

sock_can_send_data is a safe state to also act in here: it only means the
tx side still believes it has more to package, which it will still go do
via bn_tx_inotify() below either way -- forcing buf_wr_index back to
wantedpos first just makes sure that next package starts from the
position the chip actually confirmed, instead of blindly continuing past
data the chip never received.

Caveat (important for whoever picks this up next): this is a real, correct
fix for a real race, confirmed to fire on real hardware. But it turned out
NOT to be the dominant cause of the SDIO throughput stalls. A follow-up
isolation test -- swapping in the vendor's own artosyn_sdio.ko kernel
module while keeping this patched daemon -- still showed zero stalls
across 22000+ writes (including a 33KB chunk) at the same throughput where
our own kernel module + this same daemon still stalled repeatedly. So the
remaining bug lives in our own kernel driver's /dev/artosyn_sdio chardev
(see 0011/0012), not the daemon. Keep this patch (it's a genuine
improvement and narrows the race window), but the next debugging session
should go back into driver/linux/bus/sdio.c, not this file.

--- a/daemon/sock_node.c
+++ b/daemon/sock_node.c
@@ -148,7 +148,33 @@ static int dev_dat_so_write_proc(sock_dev* psock, struct usbpack* pack)
                     retmsg.wanted_pos);
             uint64_t wantedpos = retmsg.wanted_pos;
             // 空闲状态
-            if (psock->sock_sta == sock_wait_usb_data) {
+            /* Confirmed on real hardware (0013-*.patch): the chip's -0x108
+             * idle-notify carrying wantedpos can arrive within ~2ms of the
+             * dropped low-level write it's reporting -- well before the
+             * client's own multi-second write timeout -- but this handler
+             * used to require sock_sta == sock_wait_usb_data specifically.
+             * _sock_dev_pack_make() (the tx-side thread) and this reply
+             * handler (the rx-side thread) race on that same field: a
+             * -0x108 landing while the tx side still holds sock_sta at
+             * sock_can_send_data (e.g. it hasn't yet discovered there's
+             * nothing left to package and flipped to sock_wait_usb_data)
+             * used to fall straight through here with no resend and no
+             * retry -- silently discarding a wantedpos that was already
+             * telling us exactly what to resend. The socket then just sat
+             * there until some *unrelated* later event (the next client
+             * write, whose sock_dev_push_data() call happens to run while
+             * sock_sta is sock_wait_usb_data) coincidentally re-armed this
+             * same check with the same stale wantedpos -- observed on real
+             * hardware to take up to ~2.5s, exactly matching bb_socket_
+             * write()'s own client-side "made no progress" timeout window.
+             * sock_can_send_data is a safe state to also act in here: it
+             * only means the tx side still believes it has more to
+             * package, which it will still go do via bn_tx_inotify() below
+             * either way -- forcing buf_wr_index back to wantedpos first
+             * just makes sure that next package starts from the position
+             * the chip actually confirmed, instead of blindly continuing
+             * from data the chip never received. */
+            if (psock->sock_sta == sock_wait_usb_data || psock->sock_sta == sock_can_send_data) {
                 // 发送位置错误
                 if (wantedpos >= psock->buf_head_index) {
                     uint32_t testpos = (uint32_t)(wantedpos - psock->buf_head_index);
